{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FvCQZLa2qbcC3z4bqJFC7E","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_1/projects/cost-verify-agent-rl/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# B-IAVR Literature Map\\\\n# Updated: 2026-05-16\\\\n# Categories: direct-competitor, method-component, background, evaluation\\\\n# Roles: predecessor, competitor, baseline, evaluation, background\\\\n\\\\npapers:\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Direction 1: Self-Verification / Self-Correction in LLM Agents\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kamoi2024self-correction-survey\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"When Can LLMs Actually Correct Their Own Mistakes? A Critical Survey of Self-Correction of LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kamoi et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2406.01297\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Foundational survey establishing that pure intrinsic self-correction is unreliable.\\\\n      Validates B-IAVR's use of grounded verification (environment re-observation) over prompted self-feedback.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tie2025correctbench\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Can LLMs Correct Themselves? A Benchmark of Self-Correction in LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tie et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025 Datasets & Benchmarks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2510.16062\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Benchmark showing self-correction has diminishing returns when applied uniformly.\\\\n      Supports B-IAVR's selective verification design over blanket correction.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: self-verification-dilemma-2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Self-Verification Dilemma: Experience-Driven Suppression of Overused Checking in LLM Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Long et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2602.03485\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Empirically validates that >90% of self-verification is confirmatory, ~2% corrective.\\\\n      Directly supports B-IAVR's core thesis that verification should be selective.\\\\n      Their experience-pool approach is complementary to B-IAVR's action-change signal.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhang2025incentivize-self-verify\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Incentivizing LLMs to Self-Verify Their Answers\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2506.01369\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Unifies answer generation and verification via single RL training process.\\\\n      Parallel to B-IAVR: they train verification into generation; B-IAVR trains when to trigger separate verification.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mar2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"MAR: Multi-Agent Reflexion Improves Reasoning Abilities in LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ozer et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2512.20845\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Multi-agent self-correction with diverse critic personas.\\\\n      Complementary: MAR improves correction quality; B-IAVR decides when to trigger correction.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ssr2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SSR: Socratic Self-Refine for Large Language Model Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Shi et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2511.10621\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Structured Socratic questioning for self-refinement.\\\\n      Complementary: improves correction step quality; B-IAVR focuses on when to invoke correction.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: verifiagent2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"VerifiAgent: a Unified Verification Agent in Language Model Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Han et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025 Findings\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2504.00406\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Unified verification agent with meta-verification + tool-based adaptive verification.\\\\n      Focuses on how to verify (tool selection); B-IAVR focuses on when to verify (policy learning).\\\\n      Complementary: VerifiAgent's verification quality could enhance B-IAVR's verification step.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Direction 2: Agent RL Training\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: feng2025gigpo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"GiGPO: Group-in-Group Policy Optimization for LLM Agent Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Feng et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2505.10978\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: predecessor\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Core training algorithm for B-IAVR. Nested group structure provides step-level credit\\\\n      assignment without learned critic. >12% over GRPO on ALFWorld, >9% on WebShop.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: selfguide2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Co-Evolution of Policy and Internal Reward for Language Agents (Self-Guide)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2604.03098\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: predecessor\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Direct predecessor. B-IAVR extends Self-Guide's co-evolving internal reward.\\\\n      The self-guidance signal is the foundation for action-change detection triggering verification.\\\\n      8% improvement over env-reward-only on ALFWorld with Qwen3-1.7B/4B.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: spear2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learn the Ropes, Then Trust the Wins: Self-imitation with Progressive Exploration for Agentic Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Qin et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.22601\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Curriculum-based self-imitation for sparse-reward agent tasks.\\\\n      Up to +16.1% on ALFWorld over GRPO. Self-imitation curriculum could augment B-IAVR training.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: wei2025webagent-r1\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"WebAgent-R1: Training Web Agents via End-to-End Multi-Turn RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wei et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2505.16421\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Extends GRPO to multi-turn (M-GRPO) with dynamic context compression.\\\\n      Qwen-2.5-3B: 6.1% to 33.9% on WebArena-Lite. M-GRPO multi-turn extension relevant to B-IAVR.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dapo2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DAPO: An Open-Source LLM Reinforcement Learning System at Scale\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2503.14476\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      RL training stabilization techniques (Clip-Higher, Dynamic Sampling).\\\\n      Applicable to B-IAVR's RL training to prevent entropy collapse.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: digiRL2024\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DigiRL: Training In-The-Wild Device-Control Agents with Autonomous RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bai et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2406.11896\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Online RL for device control agents. Demonstrates online RL viability for interactive agents.\\\\n      49.5% absolute improvement over SFT. Parallel approach to B-IAVR in text-based setting.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: li2025sorl\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SORL: Stabilizing Off-Policy RL for Long-Horizon Agent Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2511.20718\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Turn-level importance sampling + clipping-triggered normalization for stable multi-turn agent RL.\\\\n      Directly applicable to B-IAVR's multi-turn ALFWorld training stability.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: agentic-rl-survey-2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"The Landscape of Agentic Reinforcement Learning for LLMs: A Survey\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"TMLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.02547\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Comprehensive survey formalizing agentic RL. Contextualizes B-IAVR as training agents\\\\n      to make meta-decisions (when to verify) via RL within the POMDP framework.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: skillmaster2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SkillMaster: Toward Autonomous Skill Mastery in LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2605.08693\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      DualAdv-GRPO: separately estimates advantages for task-solving and skill-editing decisions.\\\\n      +8.8% on ALFWorld, +9.3% on WebShop. DualAdv structure could inform B-IAVR's\\\\n      separation of action and verification advantages.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: t2po2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"T2PO: Uncertainty-Guided Exploration Control for Stable Multi-Turn Agentic RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2026 Spotlight\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2605.02178\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Token- and turn-level uncertainty monitoring for stable multi-turn agent RL.\\\\n      Triggers thinking intervention when marginal uncertainty change drops below threshold.\\\\n      Evaluated on ALFWorld and WebShop. Conceptually related: uncertainty-based trigger vs\\\\n      B-IAVR's action-change trigger, but T2PO targets training stability, not deployment verification.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gvpo2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"GVPO: Group Verification-based Policy Optimization for Interactive Coding Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Dai et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: null  # no arXiv preprint; OpenReview ID: RY47Tq0VsV\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Advantage shaping with outcome-verifiable + process-verifiable signals.\\\\n      +12.7% over o1 on AppWorld. Integrates intermediate execution feedback as step-level\\\\n      verification signals. Different focus: uses verification for credit assignment during\\\\n      training; B-IAVR learns when to invoke verification at deployment.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: agentv-rl2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AgentV-RL: Scaling Reward Modeling with Agentic Verifier\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2604.16004\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Bidirectional forward/backward agentic verifier for reward modeling in RL.\\\\n      4B model surpasses SOTA ORMs by 25.2%. Addresses verification quality in reward modeling;\\\\n      B-IAVR addresses verification timing in agent deployment. Complementary.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: pa-moe2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"PA-MoE: Phase-Aware Mixture of Experts for Agentic Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2602.17038\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] Lightweight phase router learns latent phase boundaries from RL objective.\\\\n      PA-MoE+GiGPO with Qwen2.5-1.5B achieves 93.8% on ALFWorld (+7.7 over GiGPO 86.1%).\\\\n      1.5B outperforms vanilla 7B GiGPO (90.2%). New small-model SOTA on ALFWorld.\\\\n      Orthogonal architecture innovation; important baseline to cite.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: sdar2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SDAR: Self-Distilled Agentic Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Lu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2605.15155\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] On-policy self-distillation as gated auxiliary objective during RL training.\\\\n      Token-level gate decides supervision intensity. +9.4% over GRPO on ALFWorld.\\\\n      Complementary: SDAR improves RL training; B-IAVR learns verification policy.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: hcapo2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"HCAPO: Hindsight Credit Assignment for Long-Horizon LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.08754\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] LLM-as-critic refines step-level Q-values via hindsight reasoning +\\\\n      multi-scale advantage for critical decision states.\\\\n      +13.8% on ALFWorld, +7.7% on WebShop over GRPO (Qwen2.5-7B).\\\\n      Could improve B-IAVR's reward signal for verification decision steps.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: act2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"ACT: Agentic Critical Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Liu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.08706\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] Trains agents to judge action quality via critical comparison reward.\\\\n      +5.07 over IL, +4.62 over RL. Different axis: ACT improves reflection quality;\\\\n      B-IAVR decides when to reflect.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rwml2026\\\\n    ti\\\\n\\\\n... [截断: 省略 13208 字符，传 max_chars=0 获取完整内容]\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 25208, \\\"truncated\\\": true}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCJRCoJ2jrVUmWHuCfPMdK","session_id":"3938fe37-c6ff-4c42-aa09-92ffcfd25d32","uuid":"e9fe261b-dc96-4bc5-bb48-a593c3b15aec","timestamp":"2026-05-17T00:16:57.638Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EsQe1WykACP9qNg4j6i8Ln","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TMa6tX3yNUNmAVFTGTKtzK","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty, positioning, and Related Work adequacy**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Learning When to Verify: Episode-Level RL and Selective Self-Verification for Interactive Agents\"\n\nThe paper presents two contributions:\n1. **Episode-level GRPO**: Identifies that step-independent RL fails for long-horizon agent tasks due to credit assignment decay (γ^(T-h) → 0 for early steps). Proposes treating entire trajectory as single decision unit. Achieves 48.2% on ALFWorld (8× over step-independent, std=2.9pp across 3 seeds).\n2. **B-IAVR**: A selective verification router trained via RL using action-change as intrinsic reward. Reduces verification by 48% at 1.4pp success cost, outperforming confidence-threshold, random, and reproduced ARES baselines.\n\n## Paper Content (9 pages)\n\n### Abstract (p1)\nSelf-verification improves multi-turn interactive agents but incurs substantial inference cost, with majority of verification calls merely confirming correct actions. Two contributions: (1) episode-level GRPO solving credit assignment decay, achieving 48.2% success on ALFWorld (8× over step-independent); (2) B-IAVR selective verification reducing calls by 48% at 1.4pp cost. Results generalize across environments (ScienceWorld 6.75×) and model scales (Qwen3-7B 7.1×).\n\n### Introduction (p1-2)\n- Interactive agents face reliability vs efficiency tension in self-verification\n- 68% of verification calls are confirmatory (no action change)\n- Step-independent RL (e.g., RAGEN) creates catastrophic credit assignment decay for T>30 step tasks\n- Episode-level GRPO treats full trajectory as single decision, assigns uniform reward to all steps\n- B-IAVR adds binary verify/skip token, trains with action-change reward\n- Contributions: (1) episode-level credit assignment with mechanistic analysis, (2) action-change as intrinsic reward, (3) B-IAVR Pareto-optimal verification reduction, (4) generalization across environments/scales/seeds\n\n### Related Work (p2)\nThree paragraphs:\n- **RL for Language Agents**: RAGEN uses step-independent GRPO; AgentTrek assumes short-horizon credit propagation. Paper claims to identify specific failure mode and proposes episode-level assignment.\n- **Self-Verification**: Self-Guide (always-on for 1.7B), ReflAct (GPT-4 prompted). Both verify every step. Paper's selective approach maintains performance while reducing cost.\n- **Adaptive Computation**: ARES (supervised router, oracle labels), Thinkless (RL control tokens for reasoning skip). B-IAVR shares RL philosophy with Thinkless, no supervised labels unlike ARES.\n\n### Method (p2-3)\n**3.1 Problem Setting**: Agent interacts with text environment over T steps, optional verification module producing corrected action a'_t. Binary success reward R∈{0,1}.\n\n**3.2 Episode-Level GRPO**: Standard step-independent assigns credit ∝ γ^(T-t). For ALFWorld T≈50, h≤10: γ^(T-h) < 0.01. Episode-level treats (a1,...,aT) as single compound action:\n∇_θ J = E[Σ_t ∇_θ log π_θ(a_t|s_t) · A_episode] where A_episode = R - R̄_group\n\n**3.3 B-IAVR**: Extends action space with v_t ∈ {0,1} (verify/skip). Action-change indicator c_t = 1[a_t ≠ a'_t | v_t=1]. Reward: r = R_task + α·(mean c_t when verified) - β·(verify_rate). Training initializes from converged episode-level checkpoint, freezes task policy, optimizes only verify/skip decision.\n\n### Experiments (p3-8)\n**4.1 Episode-Level vs Step-Independent** (Tables 1-3):\n- h-sweep: h∈{2,5,10,20,full}. Sharp cliff at h≈8. h=2: 1.5%, h=full: 48.1%\n- Multi-seed: 48.2±2.9pp across seeds 42/123/7\n- Gradient signal: r=0.97 correlation with performance, 142× signal ratio full vs h=2\n\n**4.2 Action-Change Analysis** (Tables 4-6):\n- 4820 verifications: 68% confirmatory, 32% corrective\n- Per-task: pick_two 45% change rate, examine 22%\n- Entropy-controlled: partial correlation r=0.27, p=0.003 after controlling entropy\n- Causal intervention: verification-triggered 50.3% vs random 36.8% vs no-change 44.1% (p<0.001)\n\n**4.3 Selective Verification** (Tables 7-9):\n- B-IAVR converges step 18, verify rate 52%, action-change when verified 58% (vs 32% always-on)\n- Full comparison: B-IAVR 46.8%/52% verify. Confidence-threshold 44.3%/53%. Random 42.1%/50%. No-verify 35.6%/0%\n- Per-task: uniform degradation, max 2.3pp (pick_two)\n\n**4.4 Reward Ablation** (Table 10):\n- Action-change 46.8%, confidence-based 45.1%, task-success-only 44.8%, random-trigger 43.7%\n\n**4.5 ARES Comparison** (Table 11):\n- B-IAVR: 46.8%/52% verify/0 labels/96h GPU\n- ARES: 44.9%/61% verify/5000 labels/24h GPU\n\n**4.6 Generalization** (Table 12):\n- ScienceWorld: 32.4% vs 4.8% (6.75×)\n- Qwen3-7B: 58.7% vs 8.3% (7.1×)\n\n**4.7 Per-Task** (Table 13): advantage correlates with episode length r=0.61\n\n**4.8 No-Verify Ablation** (Table 14): 35.6% vs 48.2%, -12.6pp\n\n### Conclusion (p8-9)\nDual contribution: episode-level training + selective verification. Limitations: text-based discrete environments only; B-IAVR needs pre-converged checkpoint (96h GPU).\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (Is episode-level GRPO a genuine contribution or an obvious extension? Is action-change reward truly novel? How does B-IAVR compare to Thinkless conceptually?)\n3. **Related Work Adequacy** (Coverage gaps? Missing comparisons? Fair positioning relative to prior work?)\n4. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty concerns, positioning issues, overclaiming)\n6. **Questions for Authors**\n7. **Missing References or Comparisons**\n8. **Minor Issues**\n9. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Is \"episode-level\" just REINFORCE with episode return, which is decades old? What is genuinely new about applying it to GRPO?\n- The credit assignment decay analysis (γ^(T-h)) is mathematically straightforward. Is the analysis itself a contribution, or just an observation?\n- How does this compare to advantage estimation methods (GAE) that also address credit assignment?\n- Is ALFWorld + ScienceWorld sufficient for the claims made?\n- The Related Work section is quite short (3 paragraphs). Is it adequate for a top venue?\n- Are there missing comparisons to other agent RL methods (e.g., SayCan, Inner Monologue, Reflexion)?\n\nA top venue accepts ~25%. Evaluate whether the novelty clears that bar."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34029,"cache_read_input_tokens":24213,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34029},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3938fe37-c6ff-4c42-aa09-92ffcfd25d32","uuid":"3bc31dc2-fa72-4566-8c31-4f386ae50b96"}
